    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "so if he's basically just a \"morally ungrounded\" opportunist, does that mean the idea that he was a \"great statesman\" is even worth discussing? like, is there any legitimate historical argument that his actions were actually for the benefit of Athens, or is that just something people say to make his betrayal sound more sophisticated than it actually was?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The argument that he was a \"great statesman\" is always controversial because \"greatness\" is often defined by moral consistency, which he utterly lacked.\n\nProponents argue that his brilliant tactical mind, persuasive rhetoric, and ability to win decisive military and political victories *for a time* are the true \"great\" aspects, independent of his loyalties.\n\nHowever, most critical views contend that while he was politically *brilliant* in terms of winning in the moment, his ultimate benefit to the state was always subordinate to the advantage he could gain for *himself*, making the \"great benefit to the state\" argument highly questionable.",
      "source": "target",
